QB.push(
{i:1,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"Your agent loop sends a request to the Messages API, and the response comes back with <code>stop_reason: \"tool_use\"</code>. Your current implementation logs the assistant's text block, executes the requested tool, and then starts a <em>fresh</em> conversation containing only the tool's output and the original user question. Customers report that the agent frequently repeats questions it already asked and loses track of which order it was discussing.",
ask:"What is the root cause of this behaviour?",
a:["The loop discards conversation history instead of appending the assistant turn and the <code>tool_result</code> to the running message list",
"The <code>stop_reason</code> should be checked for <code>end_turn</code> rather than <code>tool_use</code>",
"The tool result is being returned without an <code>is_error</code> flag, so Claude cannot interpret it",
"The system prompt is not being re-sent on each iteration of the loop"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The Messages API is stateless: every request must carry the full prior history, including the assistant message containing the <code>tool_use</code> block and the user message containing the matching <code>tool_result</code>.",
"<code>tool_use</code> is exactly the right signal to continue the loop; checking <code>end_turn</code> instead would terminate the loop prematurely, a different bug.",
"<code>is_error</code> only matters when a tool actually fails; its absence does not cause amnesia across turns.",
"The <code>system</code> parameter is a separate top-level field. Even if it were dropped you would see tone and policy drift, not loss of order-specific facts."],
e:"The Claude Messages API holds no server-side session state. Each request is a complete snapshot of the conversation, so the agentic loop's core contract is: append the assistant message that contained the <code>tool_use</code> block, then append a user message whose content is a <code>tool_result</code> block with a matching <code>tool_use_id</code>, then re-send the whole array. Rebuilding a minimal conversation from scratch throws away every fact Claude established earlier, which is exactly the symptom described &mdash; repeated questions and lost order context.",
k:"Agentic loop contract: send full history &rarr; read <code>stop_reason</code> &rarr; if <code>tool_use</code>, append assistant turn + <code>tool_result</code> user turn &rarr; re-send. The API is stateless; history is your job."},

{i:2,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"A developer writes an agent loop that terminates when the assistant's text output contains the phrase <em>Task complete</em>. In production the loop sometimes stops halfway through a multi-step task because Claude wrote &ldquo;once this step is done the task will be complete&rdquo;, and sometimes never stops at all because Claude phrased the ending differently.",
ask:"What is the correct way to control loop termination?",
a:["Terminate when the API response returns <code>stop_reason: \"end_turn\"</code> and continue while it returns <code>tool_use</code>",
"Ask Claude in the system prompt to always emit the exact literal string <code>TASK_COMPLETE</code> and keep string matching on that",
"Cap the loop at a fixed number of iterations and treat exhaustion as completion",
"Add a <code>finish_task</code> tool and terminate as soon as Claude calls it"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. <code>stop_reason</code> is a structural field on the response and is the intended, deterministic control signal for an agentic loop.",
"A more reliable sentinel is still a probabilistic instruction. It reduces the failure rate but does not eliminate it, and it ignores the field the API already gives you for free.",
"Iteration caps are a necessary <em>safety net</em> against runaway loops, but using exhaustion as the definition of success means a task that finishes in 3 steps still burns the full budget and a task needing 12 steps is silently truncated.",
"Plausible and sometimes useful for explicit sign-off workflows, but redundant here: <code>end_turn</code> already tells you Claude has stopped requesting tools. It also adds a tool Claude may forget to call."],
e:"The exam repeatedly tests the distinction between <strong>structural signals</strong> the API guarantees and <strong>textual signals</strong> the model produces probabilistically. <code>stop_reason</code> is structural: <code>\"tool_use\"</code> means Claude wants a tool executed and the loop must continue; <code>\"end_turn\"</code> means Claude finished its turn naturally and the loop must stop. Parsing assistant prose for completion markers is the canonical anti-pattern because model phrasing varies. Iteration caps still belong in the code &mdash; just as a circuit breaker, not as the primary stopping mechanism.",
k:"Never parse assistant text to decide control flow. <code>stop_reason</code> drives the loop; iteration caps are a safety net, not a termination condition."},

{i:3,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"In one turn, Claude returns an assistant message containing three separate <code>tool_use</code> blocks: <code>lookup_order</code>, <code>get_customer</code> and <code>check_inventory</code>. Your loop executes them, then sends three consecutive user messages back &mdash; one <code>tool_result</code> per message. The API returns a 400 error.",
ask:"What is wrong with this implementation?",
a:["All <code>tool_result</code> blocks for a single assistant turn must be returned together in the content array of one user message",
"You may only execute one tool per turn; Claude should never emit three <code>tool_use</code> blocks",
"Each <code>tool_result</code> needs its own assistant message inserted between the user messages",
"The tools must be executed strictly in the order Claude listed them, and the error indicates an ordering violation"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. One assistant turn maps to exactly one user turn carrying every corresponding <code>tool_result</code> block, each matched by <code>tool_use_id</code>.",
"Parallel tool use is a supported and desirable feature. Claude emitting several independent <code>tool_use</code> blocks in one turn is how you avoid serial round trips.",
"Messages must alternate user/assistant. Inserting synthetic assistant messages between tool results breaks the alternation and the <code>tool_use_id</code> pairing.",
"Execution order of independent parallel tools is your choice; the API does not validate it. The 400 is about message structure."],
e:"When Claude issues parallel tool calls, the conversation shape is: one assistant message with N <code>tool_use</code> blocks, followed by <strong>one</strong> user message whose <code>content</code> array holds N <code>tool_result</code> blocks, each carrying the <code>tool_use_id</code> it answers. Splitting them across multiple user messages violates the required user/assistant alternation and leaves <code>tool_use</code> blocks unanswered, which the API rejects. Practically, this is also where parallelism pays off: run the three tools concurrently, then bundle the results.",
k:"N <code>tool_use</code> blocks in one assistant message &rarr; N <code>tool_result</code> blocks in <strong>one</strong> user message, matched by <code>tool_use_id</code>. Never split them."},

{i:4,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"Your support agent occasionally returns a response with <code>stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"</code> in the middle of emitting a <code>tool_use</code> block. Your loop currently treats any non-<code>end_turn</code> response as &ldquo;continue&rdquo; and tries to execute whatever tool input it managed to parse, which has produced refunds with truncated, malformed arguments.",
ask:"How should the loop handle <code>stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"</code>?",
a:["Treat it as an incomplete turn: do not execute the partial tool call, and retry the turn with a higher <code>max_tokens</code> or a reduced request",
"Execute the tool with whatever arguments parsed successfully and let the tool validate the rest",
"Immediately escalate every occurrence to a human agent without retrying",
"Append an empty <code>tool_result</code> and let Claude decide what to do next"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. <code>max_tokens</code> means the output was cut off mid-generation, so the tool call is untrustworthy and must not be executed.",
"This is the bug being described. Partially generated JSON is not a valid tool input, and for a financial operation like <code>process_refund</code> it is actively dangerous.",
"Over-correcting. Truncation is a transient, self-inflicted configuration issue that retrying usually fixes; routing every occurrence to a human destroys first-contact resolution for no benefit.",
"There is no completed <code>tool_use</code> block to answer, so a <code>tool_result</code> would have no valid <code>tool_use_id</code> to reference."],
e:"<code>stop_reason</code> has more values than <code>end_turn</code> and <code>tool_use</code>, and an agent loop must branch on all of them. <code>max_tokens</code> means generation hit the output ceiling, so the final content block &mdash; often a <code>tool_use</code> with half-written JSON &mdash; is incomplete. The safe handling is to discard the partial turn and retry with more headroom, or with a trimmed request. Executing partial arguments against a state-changing tool is precisely the class of failure the exam wants you to design out.",
k:"Branch on every <code>stop_reason</code>: <code>end_turn</code> stop &middot; <code>tool_use</code> continue &middot; <code>max_tokens</code> incomplete, retry &middot; <code>stop_sequence</code> hit a configured stop &middot; <code>pause_turn</code> resume a long-running server tool."},

{i:5,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"An engineer proposes replacing your model-driven agent loop with a hard-coded decision tree: classify the user's intent with a small model, then run a fixed sequence of tool calls per intent. The workload is open-ended technical support where users routinely combine several unrelated problems in one message.",
ask:"What is the strongest architectural argument against the hard-coded decision tree here?",
a:["Fixed decision trees cannot adapt when a request combines intents or when an intermediate tool result changes what should happen next",
"Decision trees are always slower than letting the model choose tools",
"Claude cannot be integrated with classifier models, so the design is not implementable",
"Hard-coded trees make token costs unpredictable"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The workload described &mdash; multi-intent, open-ended, with branching that depends on intermediate results &mdash; is exactly where model-driven tool selection beats a static graph.",
"Not true as a rule. A fixed path can be faster because it skips deliberation; latency is not the deciding factor.",
"Hybrid designs using a router model in front of Claude are perfectly implementable and are sometimes the right answer for narrow, high-volume, well-bounded flows.",
"The opposite is closer to true: fixed pipelines have <em>more</em> predictable cost than open-ended loops. Cost is not the argument."],
e:"The trade-off is determinism versus adaptability. Hard-coded pipelines are the right call for narrow, well-understood, high-volume flows where you need auditable, repeatable behaviour. Model-driven loops earn their keep when the space of valid next actions depends on what the previous tool returned, and when a single user message legitimately spans several intents. Note the exam's nuance: this is not &ldquo;model-driven is always better&rdquo;. It is &ldquo;match the control strategy to how much branching the domain actually has&rdquo;.",
k:"Fixed pipeline vs agentic loop: choose fixed when the steps are known in advance; choose model-driven when the next step depends on intermediate results."},

{i:6,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"Your agent loop currently has no upper bound on iterations. During an incident, one conversation consumed 340 tool calls in a retry cycle: a flaky <code>lookup_order</code> tool returned a timeout, Claude retried it, it timed out again, and the pattern continued until the request was killed manually.",
ask:"Which combination of changes addresses this most effectively?",
a:["Add a maximum-iteration circuit breaker <em>and</em> return a structured non-retryable error from the tool after its own retry budget is exhausted",
"Add a maximum-iteration circuit breaker only, and leave the tool behaviour unchanged",
"Instruct Claude in the system prompt never to call the same tool more than twice in a row",
"Increase the tool timeout so that the calls succeed instead of failing"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The circuit breaker bounds the blast radius; the structured error tells Claude the failure is terminal so it stops retrying and moves to escalation instead.",
"The breaker alone stops the runaway but wastes the whole budget on a doomed tool and then ends the conversation with no useful outcome for the customer.",
"A prompt instruction gives probabilistic compliance. It will hold most of the time and fail exactly during the incident conditions you are trying to defend against.",
"Treats the symptom of one flaky dependency. It does nothing for the general case where a tool is genuinely down, and long timeouts make the runaway slower rather than rarer."],
e:"Runaway loops usually have two independent defects: no bound on the loop, and no signal telling the model that retrying is pointless. Fix both. The circuit breaker is code-level and deterministic. The structured error &mdash; carrying something like <code>errorCategory: \"transient\"</code> with <code>isRetryable: false</code> once the tool's own retry budget is spent &mdash; converts an ambiguous failure into an actionable one, letting Claude route to <code>escalate_to_human</code> with context rather than spinning.",
k:"Bound the loop in code <em>and</em> make failure states legible to the model. A breaker without a good error message just fails more tidily."},

{i:7,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"MAR",diff:3,
q:"A research agent must gather sources, read them, and write a report. Your loop implementation appends tool results but truncates the conversation to the last 8 messages before each API call to control cost. The agent now writes reports that contradict sources it read earlier in the same run.",
ask:"What is the most appropriate fix?",
a:["Persist key findings to an external store or scratchpad and inject a compact facts block, rather than blindly truncating recent history",
"Remove the truncation entirely and always send the full history regardless of length",
"Lower the temperature so the model contradicts itself less often",
"Switch the report-writing step to a separate model with a larger context window"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Sliding-window truncation deletes evidence by position, not by importance. Externalising findings preserves what matters at a fraction of the token cost.",
"Tempting, and correct for short runs, but it just moves the failure: eventually you exhaust the context window and hit the same class of problem with worse economics.",
"Temperature affects sampling variance, not whether the evidence is present in the context. The model is not being random; it is being uninformed.",
"A bigger window buys headroom but does not solve the design flaw, and the report writer still needs the findings passed to it."],
e:"A naive sliding window is the classic cost optimisation that quietly destroys reliability, because relevance and recency are not the same thing. The durable pattern is to extract the load-bearing facts &mdash; findings, figures, source URLs, decisions &mdash; into a structured block that is re-injected on every turn, or into a scratchpad file the agent can re-read. That block is small, stable, and immune to being scrolled out. Trimming verbose tool payloads down to the fields you actually need is the companion optimisation.",
k:"Never trim context by position alone. Extract critical facts into a persistent block; trim verbose tool output by field."},

{i:8,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"Your tool executor catches every exception and returns the string <code>\"Error\"</code> as the <code>tool_result</code> content, with <code>is_error</code> unset. Claude's subsequent behaviour is erratic: sometimes it retries, sometimes it invents a plausible-sounding value and continues, sometimes it apologises and stops.",
ask:"What is the most effective change?",
a:["Return <code>is_error: true</code> with a specific message describing what failed and whether a retry could succeed",
"Return an empty <code>tool_result</code> so Claude does not anchor on the error string",
"Raise the exception out of the loop and fail the whole request",
"Add a system prompt rule telling Claude to always retry once when it sees the word Error"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The <code>is_error</code> flag plus a specific, actionable message is what lets the model choose between retry, alternative path, and escalation.",
"An empty result is worse: it is indistinguishable from a legitimate no-results response, which is exactly the ambiguity that makes models fabricate.",
"Sometimes right for unrecoverable faults, but as a blanket policy it discards the model's ability to recover locally &mdash; a single flaky call kills an otherwise salvageable conversation.",
"A prompt rule on top of an uninformative signal. Retrying a validation error will never succeed, so the rule creates a new failure mode."],
e:"Generic error strings are one of the exam's favourite anti-patterns because they collapse several very different situations into one indistinguishable signal. A permission denial, a malformed argument, a timeout and an empty result set all demand different next actions. Marking the result with <code>is_error: true</code> and describing the failure concretely &mdash; what was attempted, why it failed, whether retrying is meaningful &mdash; gives the model the information it needs to make the right call, and gives your logs something to alert on.",
k:"<code>is_error: true</code> + specific, actionable message. Distinguish transient / validation / permission / business-rule failures, and empty results from access failures."},

{i:9,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"DPT",diff:2,
q:"An agent that explores codebases takes 40&ndash;60 seconds per user request. Tracing shows it issues one tool call per turn: <code>Glob</code> to find files, wait, <code>Grep</code> for a symbol, wait, <code>Read</code> a file, wait. The three initial discovery calls have no dependency on each other.",
ask:"Which change most directly reduces latency?",
a:["Prompt the agent to batch independent discovery calls so they are emitted as parallel <code>tool_use</code> blocks in a single turn",
"Reduce <code>max_tokens</code> so each turn generates faster",
"Merge <code>Glob</code>, <code>Grep</code> and <code>Read</code> into one general-purpose <code>explore</code> tool",
"Cache tool results so repeated calls return instantly"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Independent calls emitted in one assistant turn execute concurrently, collapsing three sequential round trips into one.",
"Shaves a little generation time but does nothing about the three model round trips, which dominate the latency.",
"Merging removes the clear input/output contracts that make tool selection reliable, and a general-purpose tool tends to be misused. It also does not guarantee parallelism.",
"Caching helps on repeats within a session, but the first exploration of an unfamiliar codebase is a cold cache by definition."],
e:"Every extra loop iteration costs a full model round trip, so latency in agentic systems is usually a function of turn count, not token count. Claude can emit multiple <code>tool_use</code> blocks in a single assistant message when the calls are independent; your executor then runs them concurrently and returns all the <code>tool_result</code> blocks in one user message. Encouraging this explicitly &mdash; &ldquo;when several lookups are independent, request them together&rdquo; &mdash; is one of the highest-leverage latency fixes available.",
k:"Latency &asymp; number of loop iterations. Batch independent tool calls into a single turn; reserve sequential turns for genuine dependencies."},

{i:10,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"Your agent handles a request where the customer asks to (1) return a damaged item, (2) dispute a duplicate charge, and (3) update their shipping address. The agent resolves the return, then ends its turn. Customers frequently come back saying two of their three issues were ignored.",
ask:"Which change most reliably fixes this?",
a:["Have the agent enumerate the distinct requests as an explicit checklist at the start, then work and verify each item before ending the turn",
"Increase the iteration limit so the loop has room to address all three",
"Add a system prompt line saying &ldquo;be thorough and address everything the customer asks&rdquo;",
"Split incoming messages into separate conversations, one per detected intent"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Making the decomposition explicit and checkable converts a vague thoroughness goal into a verifiable state the agent can reason against before finishing.",
"The loop is not running out of room; it is deciding it is finished. More headroom changes nothing.",
"Vague exhortations are exactly the kind of instruction the exam contrasts with explicit criteria. It will help marginally and fail unpredictably.",
"Fragments the customer's context. The address change may be relevant to the return shipping label, and the customer now gets three disjoint conversations."],
e:"Multi-aspect requests fail when the agent has no representation of &ldquo;what is still outstanding&rdquo;. The fix is structural: decompose first, make the list explicit in the working context, then verify each item is resolved or escalated before ending the turn. This is the agentic loop's <em>verify results</em> phase applied to task coverage rather than to output quality, and it generalises &mdash; the same pattern fixes research agents that cover only one subtopic and review agents that check only the first file.",
k:"Gather context &rarr; take action &rarr; <strong>verify results</strong>. Decompose multi-part requests into an explicit checklist and verify coverage before <code>end_turn</code>."},

{i:11,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"You are deciding where to enforce that an agent never calls <code>delete_records</code> on a production database.",
ask:"Which mechanism gives you a guarantee rather than a strong tendency?",
a:["Withhold the tool from that agent's tool list, or block the call in a hook or permission rule",
"Write an emphatic instruction in the system prompt in capital letters",
"Provide few-shot examples showing the agent declining to delete production records",
"Set <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>auto</code> so Claude is free to avoid the tool"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. If the tool is not in the schema list, the model cannot call it; if a hook or permission rule denies it, your code refuses regardless of what the model wants.",
"Emphasis raises compliance probability. It never reaches certainty, and adversarial or confusing inputs are precisely when it fails.",
"Few-shot examples shape the distribution of behaviour. They are a prompt technique and inherit the same probabilistic ceiling.",
"<code>auto</code> merely lets the model decide whether to call a tool. It permits the deletion rather than preventing it."],
e:"This is the single most repeated principle in the Architect exam: <strong>prompts give probabilistic compliance, code gives deterministic guarantees</strong>. Whenever a question mentions money, deletion, identity verification, compliance thresholds or irreversible actions, the correct answer lives in the enforcement layer &mdash; tool allow-lists, permission rules, hooks, or preconditions in the tool implementation &mdash; not in the wording of a prompt. Prompts remain the right tool for judgement, tone and prioritisation.",
k:"Guarantee &rarr; code (allow-list, hook, precondition). Tendency &rarr; prompt. Anything irreversible or regulated belongs in code."},

{i:12,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"SDE",diff:2,
q:"An extraction agent processes a document, calls a <code>save_extraction</code> tool, receives a success result, and then &mdash; in the same conversation &mdash; calls <code>save_extraction</code> again with the same payload. Duplicate rows are appearing in the database.",
ask:"Which fix is most robust?",
a:["Make <code>save_extraction</code> idempotent by accepting a caller-supplied key and returning the existing record on repeat calls",
"Add a prompt instruction telling the agent to call <code>save_extraction</code> exactly once per document",
"Remove <code>save_extraction</code> from the agent and have application code persist the result after the loop ends",
"Lower the temperature to reduce repeated tool calls"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Idempotency makes the duplicate call harmless, which is the only defence that survives retries, network replays and model quirks alike.",
"A reasonable supporting measure, but it is a probabilistic instruction defending a data-integrity invariant. The exam consistently marks this insufficient on its own.",
"Genuinely viable and arguably cleaner for a pure extraction pipeline &mdash; but it removes the agent's ability to save incrementally during multi-document runs, and duplicates could still arise from application-level retries.",
"Temperature is unrelated. Duplicate tool calls arise from planning and retry behaviour, not sampling noise."],
e:"Agents retry. Networks replay. Loops re-enter. Any tool that changes state should therefore be safe to call twice &mdash; typically by accepting an idempotency key (document id, request id) and returning the prior result rather than creating a second record. This is a tool-design responsibility, not a prompting responsibility, and it holds for refunds, ticket creation, emails and writes alike. Notice that option C is not wrong in principle; it is simply narrower than making the tool safe.",
k:"Every state-changing tool an agent can call should be idempotent. Assume the model will call it twice, because eventually it will."},

{i:13,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"CAP",diff:3,
q:"In a long multi-turn conversation, your agent stops honouring a formatting instruction the user gave in turn 2 (&ldquo;always reply in bullet points, never prose&rdquo;). By turn 40 it is writing paragraphs again. The full history is being sent on every request.",
ask:"What is the most effective architectural fix?",
a:["Move the durable instruction into the system prompt so it is re-asserted at a privileged, stable position on every request",
"Repeat the instruction as a user message after every assistant reply",
"Increase <code>max_tokens</code> so the model has room to comply",
"Ask the user to restate their formatting preference whenever it drifts"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The <code>system</code> parameter is the designated home for standing instructions and is not competing with 40 turns of intervening conversation.",
"It works, but it bloats the transcript, costs tokens on every turn, and clutters the conversation the user sees. It treats a placement problem with repetition.",
"Output length is unrelated to instruction persistence. The model is not truncating; it is deprioritising.",
"Pushes the system's design flaw onto the user, which the exam treats as a non-answer."],
e:"Instructions buried in conversational history compete with everything that came after them and steadily lose salience &mdash; the practical face of the lost-in-the-middle effect. Anything that must hold for the whole session belongs in the system prompt, which sits at a fixed, privileged position on every request. The general rule: <em>durable</em> constraints go in the system prompt (or a persisted preferences block your code injects); <em>situational</em> requests stay in the conversation.",
k:"Durable instruction &rarr; system prompt or an injected persistent block. Turn-specific request &rarr; conversation. Never rely on turn-2 instructions surviving to turn 40."},

{i:14,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"You are reviewing an agent loop and notice it sends <code>tools</code> in the first request only, omitting the parameter on subsequent iterations to save tokens. After the first tool call, Claude stops calling tools entirely and starts describing what it would do instead.",
ask:"Why is this happening?",
a:["Tool definitions must be supplied on every request; without them Claude has no tools available to call",
"Tool definitions are cached server-side for 5 minutes, so this only fails on slow conversations",
"Omitting <code>tools</code> implicitly sets <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>none</code> for that turn only",
"The <code>tool_result</code> blocks in history are invalid without matching definitions, so the API silently drops them"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Like the message history, the tool schema list is part of every stateless request. Omit it and the model genuinely has no tools.",
"There is no implicit server-side caching of tool definitions. Prompt caching exists but is explicit, opt-in via <code>cache_control</code>, and still requires you to send the content.",
"Invented behaviour. <code>tool_choice: none</code> is something you set deliberately.",
"Historic <code>tool_result</code> blocks remain valid; the problem is forward-looking, not retrospective."],
e:"Statelessness applies to the whole request, not just to <code>messages</code>. The <code>system</code> prompt, the <code>tools</code> array and the message history must all be re-sent every iteration. If token cost of a large tool schema is genuinely a concern, the right lever is <strong>prompt caching</strong>: mark the stable prefix &mdash; system prompt plus tool definitions &mdash; with <code>cache_control</code> so repeat sends are billed at a large discount, rather than dropping the content entirely.",
k:"Every request re-sends system prompt + tools + full history. To cut the cost of that repetition, use prompt caching on the stable prefix &mdash; never omission."},

{i:15,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"Your agent must verify a customer's identity before any account change. Today the system prompt says: <em>Always call <code>get_customer</code> and confirm the verified flag before calling <code>process_refund</code>.</em> An audit finds that in 12% of refunds, <code>process_refund</code> was called without a preceding successful <code>get_customer</code>.",
ask:"Which change is most effective?",
a:["Enforce the precondition in code: have <code>process_refund</code> reject any call when the session has no verified customer id",
"Rewrite the system prompt with stronger, more explicit wording about the verification requirement",
"Add few-shot examples showing the correct <code>get_customer</code> &rarr; <code>process_refund</code> sequence",
"Introduce an intent classifier that routes refund requests to a dedicated refund agent"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A hard business invariant enforced at the tool boundary cannot be talked around, regardless of how the conversation unfolds.",
"Better prompts reduce the 12% but cannot drive it to zero. For a financial control, any residual rate is an audit finding.",
"Few-shot examples shape typical behaviour and are useful, but they are still guidance. Unusual phrasing will still route around them.",
"Routing addresses which agent handles the request, not whether that agent respects the ordering constraint. The same gap reappears inside the specialised agent."],
e:"This mirrors an officially published sample question and is the archetype for the whole exam. When a specific tool ordering carries business or regulatory weight, implement a <strong>programmatic precondition</strong>: the downstream tool inspects session state and refuses to run until the upstream step has completed successfully. Equivalent implementations include an Agent SDK <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks the call, or a permission rule. Prompt-side techniques (B and C) are worth adding as a second layer, because a blocked call still produces a better conversation if the model understood the rule &mdash; but they are never the answer to a hard invariant.",
k:"Ordering that carries business risk &rarr; precondition in the tool or a blocking hook. Prompts are defence in depth, never the control itself."},

{i:16,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"A teammate argues that because Claude decides which tool to call, the agent loop does not need any application-level logging beyond the final answer.",
ask:"What is the strongest counter-argument?",
a:["Without a per-iteration record of tool calls, inputs, results and <code>stop_reason</code>, you cannot diagnose why a run went wrong or reproduce it",
"Logging is required for the API to function correctly",
"Claude cannot see its own tool calls, so logs are the only way it can learn from them",
"Logs are needed because the Messages API charges per log entry"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The tool trace is the only observable record of the agent's reasoning path and is the primary debugging artefact for agentic systems.",
"The API has no logging requirement; this is an operational concern, not a functional one.",
"Claude does see its own prior tool calls &mdash; they are in the conversation history you re-send. And no learning happens from logs at inference time.",
"Fabricated billing model."],
e:"Agentic systems fail in ways that only the trace can explain: the model picked the wrong tool because two descriptions overlapped, a tool returned an ambiguous empty result, a hook silently blocked a call, the loop hit an iteration cap. Capturing every iteration &mdash; tool name, arguments, result, latency, <code>stop_reason</code>, token usage &mdash; is what makes those diagnosable. This is also why hub-and-spoke multi-agent designs route all communication through the coordinator: a single choke point where the whole trace is observable.",
k:"Log every loop iteration: tool, args, result, <code>stop_reason</code>, tokens. In multi-agent systems, route messages through the coordinator so there is one observable trace."},

{i:17,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"DPT",diff:2,
q:"An agent is asked to &ldquo;find where user authentication happens and explain the flow&rdquo; in a 400,000-line codebase it has never seen. It immediately calls <code>Read</code> on twelve large files it guessed by name, exhausting a large share of the context window before finding anything relevant.",
ask:"Which strategy should the agent follow instead?",
a:["Search first with <code>Grep</code> and <code>Glob</code> to locate candidates, then <code>Read</code> only the specific files and ranges that the search identified",
"Read every file in the repository once so the agent has complete information",
"Ask the user to paste the relevant authentication code into the conversation",
"Increase the context window and continue reading whole files"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Cheap, targeted search narrows the space before expensive full-file reads consume context.",
"Catastrophic at 400,000 lines and unnecessary &mdash; the vast majority of the repository is irrelevant to the question.",
"Defeats the purpose of a codebase-exploration agent and assumes the user already knows the answer.",
"Buys headroom but keeps the wasteful access pattern; you will hit the same wall on a larger repository."],
e:"Incremental investigation is the expected pattern: use <code>Grep</code> to search file <em>contents</em> for symbols, routes or error strings; use <code>Glob</code> to find files by name or extension pattern; only then <code>Read</code> the narrow set that matters, ideally with an offset and limit rather than whole files. Guess-and-read burns the scarcest resource in the system. For genuinely broad sweeps, delegate the discovery to a subagent so the verbose output never enters the main context at all.",
k:"Grep = search contents. Glob = find files by name pattern. Read = targeted, after search narrows the field. Never guess-and-read at scale."},

{i:18,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"CAP",diff:3,
q:"A user says: &ldquo;Cancel my subscription &mdash; actually no, first tell me what the cancellation fee would be, and if it's over $20 then cancel it anyway.&rdquo; Your agent has a <code>get_cancellation_quote</code> tool and a <code>cancel_subscription</code> tool.",
ask:"What is the correct sequencing behaviour for the agent?",
a:["Call <code>get_cancellation_quote</code>, report the fee, and only call <code>cancel_subscription</code> once the stated condition has been evaluated against the actual quote",
"Call <code>cancel_subscription</code> immediately, since the user's final instruction was to cancel",
"Call both tools in parallel in a single turn to minimise latency",
"Ask the user to restate their request as a single unambiguous instruction"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The second tool call is conditionally dependent on the first tool's result, so it must be sequential and gated on evaluating the condition.",
"Misreads a conditional as an unconditional. If the fee is $8, cancelling violates the user's actual instruction.",
"Parallelism is only valid for <em>independent</em> calls. Here the decision to cancel depends on the quote, and the action is irreversible.",
"Unnecessary friction. The request is self-consistent and machine-interpretable &mdash; a conditional with a clear threshold."],
e:"Two rules meet here. First, parallel tool calls are for independent operations; a data-dependency &mdash; especially one feeding an irreversible action &mdash; forces sequential turns. Second, self-corrections and conditionals in natural language must be resolved against the <em>latest</em> stated intent, not the first thing said. The exam distinguishes genuine ambiguity (multiple customer records match; policy is silent) from an instruction that is merely conditional. Only the former warrants asking a clarifying question.",
k:"Parallelise only independent calls. Conditional and irreversible actions are sequential and gated. Conditional &ne; ambiguous &mdash; don't ask when the rule is stated."},

{i:19,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"Your loop passes <code>tool_choice: {\"type\": \"any\"}</code> on every iteration to guarantee the agent keeps making progress. The agent now never produces a final natural-language answer &mdash; it calls a tool on every turn until the iteration cap fires.",
ask:"What went wrong?",
a:["<code>any</code> forces a tool call on every turn, so the model can never emit a plain text answer or return <code>end_turn</code>",
"<code>any</code> is invalid for multi-turn conversations and should have raised an API error",
"The iteration cap is set too low for the workload",
"<code>any</code> only works when exactly one tool is defined"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. <code>any</code> means &ldquo;you must call one of the available tools&rdquo;, which structurally prevents a text-only turn.",
"It is valid; it simply does what it says. No error is raised.",
"Raising the cap only makes the runaway longer. The loop can never terminate naturally under <code>any</code>.",
"<code>any</code> works with any number of tools &mdash; it constrains the model to pick one of them."],
e:"Know the three <code>tool_choice</code> modes cold. <code>auto</code> lets Claude decide between text and a tool call &mdash; the right default for a conversational agent loop. <code>any</code> forces some tool call, which is ideal for guaranteeing structured output but fatal as a standing setting in a loop that needs to finish. <code>{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"...\"}</code> forces one specific tool, used for single-shot extraction. A common correct pattern is <code>any</code> or a forced tool on the <em>first</em> turn to guarantee a structured start, then <code>auto</code> thereafter.",
k:"<code>auto</code> = model chooses (default for loops) &middot; <code>any</code> = must call some tool &middot; <code>{type:tool,name}</code> = must call that tool. <code>any</code> in a loop prevents termination."},

{i:20,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"Latency analysis of your support agent shows a median of 4 tool-calling iterations per conversation, but a p95 of 19. Inspecting the long runs, the agent calls <code>get_customer</code>, then <code>lookup_order</code>, then <code>get_customer</code> again, then <code>lookup_order</code> again with slightly different arguments, apparently unsure whether it already has what it needs.",
ask:"Which change most directly reduces the p95?",
a:["Return complete, well-labelled results from each tool so the agent can tell that it already has the information it needs",
"Reduce the iteration cap to 8 so long runs are cut short",
"Add a second model to review the agent's plan before each tool call",
"Move the agent to a smaller, faster model to reduce per-iteration latency"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Re-querying is a symptom of results that are ambiguous, partial or hard to map back to the question asked.",
"Truncates the symptom and turns slow conversations into failed ones. The agent still does not know what it has.",
"Doubles the model calls per iteration to solve a problem caused by poor tool output. This is the over-engineering distractor.",
"Smaller models are generally <em>worse</em> at recognising they already have sufficient information, so p95 iteration count is likely to rise."],
e:"Repeated near-identical tool calls almost always trace back to the tool contract rather than the model. Either the result omits the field the agent needed, or it is unlabelled enough that the agent cannot match it to its goal, or an empty result is indistinguishable from a failure. Returning clearly structured results &mdash; with the query echoed back, explicit field names, and an unambiguous marker for &ldquo;found nothing, and that is a valid answer&rdquo; &mdash; is what collapses the retry loop. Trim verbosity, but do not trim the fields the agent is actually reasoning over.",
k:"Repeated tool calls = a tool-output problem, not a model problem. Make results complete, labelled, and unambiguous about empty vs failed."},

{i:21,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"Your research system has a coordinator plus four specialised subagents. To reduce latency, an engineer wires the web-search subagent to call the document-analysis subagent directly when it finds a PDF, bypassing the coordinator. Within a week, nobody can explain why certain reports contain sources that were never requested.",
ask:"What principle does this design violate?",
a:["In hub-and-spoke orchestration the coordinator owns all inter-agent communication, which is what makes the system observable and controllable",
"Subagents are technically incapable of invoking other subagents",
"Direct subagent-to-subagent calls always cost more tokens than routing through the coordinator",
"Only the coordinator is permitted to use MCP tools"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Centralising communication at the hub is precisely what preserves traceability, error handling and routing control.",
"It is usually technically possible &mdash; that is why the discipline matters. The constraint is architectural, not physical.",
"Cost is not the issue, and routing through a hub often costs slightly more. Observability and control are the point.",
"No such restriction exists; subagents routinely hold their own scoped MCP tools."],
e:"Hub-and-spoke is the reference multi-agent topology for this exam. The coordinator decomposes the task, delegates, aggregates results, handles errors and decides on re-routing. Every message crosses the hub. The moment spokes talk to each other you get a mesh: emergent behaviour nobody designed, no single trace to debug, no consistent place to apply error handling or policy, and no reliable way to detect duplicated or missing coverage. If a spoke genuinely needs a capability constantly, the correct fix is to give that spoke a narrow, scoped tool of its own &mdash; not to let it summon another agent.",
k:"Hub-and-spoke: all inter-agent traffic goes through the coordinator. Need a shortcut? Give the subagent a narrow scoped tool, don't build a mesh."},

{i:22,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:3,
q:"Every subagent in your research system completes successfully. The web-search agent returns relevant articles, the document-analysis agent summarises them correctly, and the synthesis agent produces coherent prose. Yet final reports on &ldquo;AI's impact on creative industries&rdquo; cover only visual art. The coordinator log shows it created three subtasks: <em>AI in digital art</em>, <em>AI in graphic design</em>, <em>AI in photography</em>.",
ask:"What is the most likely root cause?",
a:["The coordinator decomposed the topic too narrowly, so music, literature and film were never assigned to anyone",
"The synthesis agent is failing to detect coverage gaps in the material it receives",
"The web-search agent is not searching thoroughly enough within its assigned subtopics",
"The document-analysis agent is filtering out non-visual sources"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The log is decisive: all three subtasks are visual-domain. The missing content was never requested.",
"Gap detection in synthesis is a useful safety net, but it is a mitigation for a decomposition failure, not the cause of one.",
"Contradicted by the evidence &mdash; each agent returned relevant results <em>for the subtopic it was given</em>.",
"Also contradicted. The agents performed correctly on their inputs; the inputs were the problem."],
e:"This mirrors a published sample question and teaches a diagnostic habit the exam rewards: when every component reports success but the outcome is wrong, suspect the <strong>task decomposition</strong>, not the workers. The coordinator's most consequential and least visible responsibility is defining the problem space. Practical defences include prompting the coordinator to enumerate the dimensions of a domain before subdividing, expressing subtasks in terms of coverage goals rather than pre-chosen subtopics, and adding a synthesis-stage coverage check that names what was <em>not</em> examined.",
k:"All subagents succeed but the result is incomplete &rarr; look at the coordinator's decomposition. Coverage failures are assignment failures."},

{i:23,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"You are splitting a research topic across four parallel subagents. Early runs show heavy duplication: three of the four returned substantially the same sources, and one important angle went uncovered.",
ask:"Which coordinator change best addresses this?",
a:["Assign each subagent an explicitly disjoint slice of the topic and state what is out of scope for it",
"Run the subagents sequentially so each can see what the previous one already found",
"Deduplicate the sources in the synthesis step and accept the wasted work",
"Increase the number of subagents so more angles are covered by chance"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Explicit, mutually exclusive scoping with stated exclusions is what prevents overlap while guaranteeing coverage.",
"Solves duplication but destroys the parallelism that motivated the design, multiplying latency by four. It is the fallback when slices genuinely cannot be made disjoint.",
"Treats the symptom after paying the full cost, and does nothing for the uncovered angle &mdash; the more serious of the two problems.",
"More agents with the same vague scoping produce more duplication, not better coverage."],
e:"Parallel delegation only pays off if the slices are disjoint and jointly exhaustive. Because subagents run with isolated context, they cannot see each other's work and will independently gravitate to the most obvious sources. The coordinator must therefore do the partitioning explicitly, and stating exclusions (&ldquo;cover music and audio; do <em>not</em> cover visual art, which another agent owns&rdquo;) is markedly more effective than stating inclusions alone. Pair this with a coverage check at synthesis time to catch gaps the partition missed.",
k:"Parallel subagents need disjoint, exhaustive slices with explicit exclusions. They cannot see each other's context, so overlap is the default."},

{i:24,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:3,
q:"The synthesis agent frequently needs to verify individual claims while merging results. Today it hands control back to the coordinator, which calls the web-search agent and then re-invokes synthesis. This adds 2&ndash;3 round trips per task and raises latency 40%. Analysis shows 85% of these checks are simple factual confirmations (a date, a name, a statistic) and 15% require genuine investigation.",
ask:"How do you reduce the overhead while preserving reliability?",
a:["Give the synthesis agent a narrow <code>verify_fact</code> tool for simple confirmations, and keep routing complex verification through the coordinator",
"Give the synthesis agent full access to the web-search agent's entire toolset",
"Batch all verification needs and return them to the coordinator once at the end",
"Pre-fetch and cache additional context around every source in case verification is needed later"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Least privilege applied to the 85% case: exactly the capability needed, nothing more, with the escalation path intact for the hard 15%.",
"Breaks separation of concerns and hands the synthesis agent a large toolset outside its specialisation &mdash; which is exactly what degrades tool-selection reliability.",
"Introduces blocking dependencies: later synthesis steps often depend on facts verified earlier, so deferring them all to the end changes the output.",
"Speculative caching cannot predict which facts will need checking, and inflates context with material that is mostly never used."],
e:"This mirrors a published sample question. The principle is <strong>least privilege sized to the common case</strong>: measure the distribution of needs, grant a minimal scoped capability that covers the bulk of it, and preserve the coordinator-mediated path for the tail. Note why the tempting option B is wrong &mdash; the exam repeatedly states that giving an agent tools outside its specialisation reduces selection reliability, and that agents perform best with a small, role-relevant toolset (roughly 4&ndash;5 tools) rather than a large general one.",
k:"Grant subagents a <em>narrow</em> tool for the common case; escalate the tail through the coordinator. Broad toolsets degrade selection reliability."},

{i:25,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"Your coordinator's prompt reads: <em>Step 1: call the search agent with the exact query the user typed. Step 2: pass all results to the analysis agent. Step 3: pass the analysis to the synthesis agent. Step 4: return the synthesis.</em> Reports are shallow, and when search returns nothing useful the coordinator continues anyway and produces an empty-feeling report.",
ask:"Which change most improves report quality?",
a:["Rewrite the coordinator prompt around goals and quality criteria &mdash; what a complete report must contain &mdash; so it can re-route and iterate when a step underdelivers",
"Add a fifth step instructing the coordinator to check whether the report is good",
"Increase the number of results the search agent returns at step 1",
"Replace the coordinator with a fixed script that calls the three agents in order"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Goal-and-criteria framing is what enables the coordinator to notice an inadequate intermediate result and act on it.",
"Bolting a check onto the end of a rigid pipeline gives the coordinator no authority or mechanism to do anything about a failed check.",
"More of the same low-quality input. If the query was wrong, more results from that query do not help.",
"Formalises the rigidity that is causing the problem, and removes the adaptability that makes an agentic coordinator worth having."],
e:"A coordinator written as a step-by-step script is just a workflow engine with a language model's overheads. The value of an agentic coordinator is judgement: recognising that search returned nothing relevant, reformulating the query, dispatching an extra subagent for an uncovered angle, or looping synthesis again after filling a gap. Prompt it in terms of the outcome (&ldquo;a complete report covers every major sub-domain, cites sources for each claim, and flags areas where evidence is thin&rdquo;) and the quality bar, then let it choose the path. Keep fixed pipelines for genuinely predictable, repeatable multi-aspect work.",
k:"Prompt coordinators with goals and quality criteria, not step lists. Rigid steps forfeit the adaptability that justifies an agentic coordinator."},

{i:26,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"A stakeholder asks why the multi-agent research system does not simply use one large agent with all sixteen tools, given that a single agent would avoid coordination overhead entirely.",
ask:"What is the strongest technical justification for the multi-agent split?",
a:["Each subagent gets an isolated context window and a small, role-relevant toolset, which improves both tool-selection reliability and context headroom",
"Multi-agent systems are always cheaper in tokens than a single agent",
"A single agent cannot call more than eight tools in one conversation",
"Only multi-agent systems can run tool calls in parallel"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Context isolation and scoped toolsets are the two concrete engineering wins, and both are stated in the blueprint.",
"Usually false. Multi-agent systems typically cost <em>more</em> tokens because context is re-established per subagent; you pay that for reliability and parallelism.",
"No such limit exists. Reliability degrades gradually as the toolset grows &mdash; it is a quality curve, not a hard cap.",
"A single agent can emit parallel <code>tool_use</code> blocks in one turn. Parallelism is not exclusive to multi-agent designs."],
e:"Be able to argue both directions. Multi-agent wins when: the work decomposes into genuinely separable specialisations; discovery output is verbose enough that isolating it protects the main context; parts can run concurrently; different roles need different tool permissions. A single agent wins when: the task is small, the steps are tightly coupled, or the coordination overhead and re-established context would exceed the benefit. The honest trade-off is more tokens and more moving parts in exchange for reliability, isolation and parallelism &mdash; and the exam expects you to name that cost rather than pretend it away.",
k:"Multi-agent buys context isolation, scoped tools and parallelism &mdash; and costs extra tokens and coordination complexity. Don't split a small, tightly coupled task."},

{i:27,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:3,
q:"Your coordinator dispatches a subagent with the prompt: <em>Analyse the documents and report back.</em> The subagent replies asking which documents, and what aspects matter. The coordinator had this information in its own context from an earlier user exchange.",
ask:"What is the underlying mistake?",
a:["Subagents run with isolated context and inherit nothing; every fact they need must be stated explicitly in the delegating prompt",
"The subagent should have been given a tool to query the coordinator's conversation history",
"The coordinator should have used <code>fork_session</code> so the subagent inherits the parent context",
"The subagent's system prompt is too restrictive and is suppressing its access to shared memory"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. This is the defining property of subagent delegation and the most common source of vague-delegation bugs.",
"Building a history-query tool is an over-engineered workaround for simply writing a complete prompt, and it lets the subagent pull in irrelevant context.",
"<code>fork_session</code> branches an existing session to explore alternatives from shared context &mdash; a different mechanism with a different purpose from spawning a scoped subagent for a delegated task.",
"There is no shared memory to suppress. Isolation is by design, not a misconfiguration."],
e:"Context isolation is the whole point of subagents &mdash; it is what keeps verbose subagent work out of the coordinator's window &mdash; but it means the delegating prompt is the subagent's <em>entire</em> world. A good delegation states the goal, the specific inputs (file paths, URLs, prior findings quoted in full rather than referenced), the output format expected, and the quality criteria. &ldquo;As discussed above&rdquo; or &ldquo;the documents&rdquo; refer to nothing. When passing prior agents' outputs, include them in full and use structured formatting to separate the data from the instructions about it.",
k:"Subagents inherit no context. The delegation prompt must carry goal + full inputs + output contract + quality bar. Never reference the parent conversation."},

{i:28,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"The synthesis agent receives findings from three subagents and produces a merged report. Reviewers complain they cannot tell which claim came from which source, so nothing can be fact-checked.",
ask:"Where should this be fixed?",
a:["Require each subagent to emit structured claim-to-source mappings, and require synthesis to preserve them through aggregation",
"Have reviewers ask the coordinator to re-run the search for any claim they want to check",
"Add a footnote at the end of the report listing every source consulted",
"Instruct the synthesis agent to write more cautiously so unverifiable claims are avoided"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Attribution must be carried as structured data from the point of collection and preserved at every aggregation step.",
"Shifts the cost to the reader and cannot reconstruct which source actually supported a given sentence.",
"A bibliography proves sources were consulted but not which claim each one supports &mdash; precisely the gap being reported.",
"Reduces the number of claims rather than making them checkable, and quietly drops findings that were perfectly valid."],
e:"Attribution is lost during summarisation unless it is structural. The pattern is: subagents return findings as records carrying the claim, the source URL or document name, a supporting quote and a date; the coordinator preserves those records when aggregating; the synthesis agent renders them with inline attribution rather than flattening them into prose. Dates matter more than people expect &mdash; without them, two figures measured in different years read as a contradiction rather than as a trend.",
k:"Provenance is structural, not stylistic. Claim &rarr; source &rarr; quote &rarr; date, carried end to end. Summarisation destroys attribution unless you design against it."},

{i:29,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:3,
q:"Two credible subagents return conflicting statistics for the same metric: a government report cites 40% growth, an industry analysis cites 12%. The discrepancy materially changes the report's conclusion.",
ask:"How should the document-analysis subagent handle this?",
a:["Complete its analysis carrying both values, explicitly annotated as conflicting with full source attribution, and let the coordinator reconcile them",
"Apply credibility heuristics to choose the more authoritative figure and footnote the discrepancy",
"Halt analysis and escalate to the coordinator to decide which source to trust before continuing",
"Report both figures without marking them as conflicting and let synthesis pick using broader context"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. It finishes its own job without blocking, preserves both values with attribution, and routes the reconciliation decision to the layer that has the broader context.",
"Silently resolving a material conflict inside a subagent hides a consequential judgement in the wrong place, and a footnote does not surface it to the decision-maker.",
"Blocking is unnecessary &mdash; the analysis can complete. It also over-involves the coordinator in routine work, the anti-pattern of escalating what you could have handled.",
"Unmarked conflicting numbers are worse than either alternative: downstream agents cannot tell there is a disagreement and will treat one as fact."],
e:"This mirrors a published sample question. Three ideas combine: complete your own scope rather than blocking; preserve rather than resolve material conflicts at the leaf level; and make the conflict machine-readable so the layer with broader context can decide. Also check whether the figures are truly contradictory &mdash; different measurement years, different definitions or different populations frequently explain apparent conflicts, which is exactly why subagent output should carry dates and definitions alongside numbers.",
k:"Conflicting sources: don't pick, don't hide, don't block. Annotate the conflict with attribution and dates, finish your scope, escalate the reconciliation."},

{i:30,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"Your coordinator waits for all four subagents to finish before doing anything. One subagent routinely takes 90 seconds while the others finish in 10, so every report waits 90 seconds. The slow agent's output is only needed for one optional appendix.",
ask:"Which redesign is most appropriate?",
a:["Start synthesis on the critical-path results as soon as they arrive, and fold the optional appendix in when the slow agent returns or omit it on timeout",
"Remove the slow subagent from the system entirely",
"Run all four subagents sequentially so the timing is predictable",
"Give the slow subagent a faster model and keep the all-or-nothing wait"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Decouple optional work from the critical path and define explicit degraded-mode behaviour on timeout.",
"Throws away genuine value to fix a scheduling problem. The appendix has readers.",
"Makes the total latency the sum rather than the max &mdash; strictly worse.",
"May help a little, but it leaves the structural flaw: one optional component still gates the entire report."],
e:"Two lessons. First, distinguish critical-path outputs from optional enrichment, and never let optional work block delivery. Second, define what &ldquo;partial success&rdquo; looks like <em>before</em> you need it: on timeout, does the report ship without the appendix, with a placeholder, or not at all? Graceful degradation with an explicit coverage annotation &mdash; a section marked <em>partial coverage: source-analysis agent timed out</em> &mdash; is far better than either an indefinite wait or a silently incomplete report that looks complete.",
k:"Separate critical path from optional enrichment. Define degraded-mode output up front and annotate partial coverage in the result."},

{i:31,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"You are designing a support system with a triage agent, a billing agent and a technical agent. A stakeholder asks whether the triage agent should also hold the refund and diagnostic tools &ldquo;just in case it can resolve something quickly&rdquo;.",
ask:"What is the best design and why?",
a:["Keep triage scoped to classification and handoff tools; a broad toolset degrades its routing reliability and blurs accountability",
"Give triage every tool so simple cases are resolved in one hop",
"Give triage read-only versions of every tool but no write tools",
"Merge all three agents into one, since specialisation adds coordination cost"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Tool-selection reliability falls as the toolset grows, and an agent given tools outside its specialisation tends to misuse them.",
"This is the tempting efficiency argument the exam sets up to be wrong: triage stops triaging and starts half-solving, and routing accuracy drops.",
"A reasonable-sounding middle ground, and occasionally right &mdash; but it still loads triage with a large toolset outside its role, which is the mechanism that degrades selection.",
"Discards the specialisation benefits (scoped permissions, focused prompts, isolated context) to avoid a modest coordination cost."],
e:"The blueprint is explicit that too many tools per agent &mdash; the illustrative contrast is 18 tools versus 4&ndash;5 &mdash; measurably reduces selection reliability, and that agents holding tools outside their specialisation tend to misuse them. The correct pattern is scoped access: role-relevant tools plus a small set of cross-role utilities. Where a specialist genuinely needs one narrow capability from another domain, give it a constrained tool for exactly that (<code>verify_fact</code>, not full web search; <code>load_document</code>, not general <code>fetch_url</code>).",
k:"Scope tools to the agent's role. Small, role-relevant toolsets select more reliably; add narrow constrained tools rather than broad general ones."},

{i:32,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"A coordinator delegates the same failing subtask to a subagent three times in a row, each time with an identical prompt, because the subagent keeps returning <code>{\"status\": \"failed\"}</code>.",
ask:"Which change breaks the cycle most effectively?",
a:["Have the subagent return structured failure context &mdash; what it attempted, why it failed, partial results, and viable alternatives &mdash; so the coordinator can change its approach",
"Have the coordinator stop retrying after the first failure and abort the workflow",
"Have the coordinator retry with exponential backoff up to five times",
"Have the subagent return success with an empty result so the workflow proceeds"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The retry loop persists because the coordinator learns nothing from the failure and therefore has no basis for doing anything different.",
"Aborting on any single failure is the opposite anti-pattern &mdash; one recoverable hiccup kills an otherwise successful workflow.",
"Backoff helps only for transient faults. If the cause is a bad query or a permission problem, five spaced retries fail identically.",
"Masking failure as success is the most dangerous option: the workflow proceeds on a false premise and the gap surfaces in the final report as a silent omission."],
e:"An identical retry can only succeed if the failure was transient. Structured error context &mdash; failure type, the query or input attempted, any partial results obtained, and suggested alternatives &mdash; converts a dead end into a decision: retry with a reformulated query, try a different source, proceed with partial coverage and annotate the gap, or escalate. Note the two named anti-patterns bracketing the correct answer: silent suppression (D) and aborting the whole workflow on one failure (B).",
k:"Failures must carry context: type, attempt, partial results, alternatives. Silent suppression and whole-workflow abort are both anti-patterns."},

{i:33,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"The coordinator receives completed results from the web-search agent and the document-analysis agent.",
ask:"What is the correct next step in a hub-and-spoke architecture?",
a:["The coordinator passes both result sets to the synthesis agent for unified integration",
"Each agent sends its results directly to the report-writing agent",
"The document-analysis agent requests the search results and merges them internally",
"The coordinator concatenates the raw outputs and returns them as the final result"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Integration is the synthesis agent's specialisation, and the coordinator is the only component that routes work to it.",
"Bypasses the hub, destroying the single observable trace and the coordinator's control over routing.",
"Puts integration in an agent specialised for analysis, and creates spoke-to-spoke coupling.",
"Concatenation is not synthesis &mdash; it produces a redundant, contradictory document with no unified narrative."],
e:"A straightforward orchestration-flow question, and the exam asks several of these. The coordinator collects, then routes to the agent whose specialisation matches the next step. Watch for the two recurring distractor shapes: bypassing the hub (B, C) and the coordinator doing specialist work itself (D). The coordinator decomposes, delegates, aggregates, handles errors and re-routes &mdash; it does not synthesise, analyse or search.",
k:"Coordinator responsibilities: decompose, delegate, aggregate, handle errors, re-route. It routes to specialists; it does not do their work."},

{i:34,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"You need to compare two competing refactoring approaches for the same module. Both need the same 20 minutes of codebase context the agent has already built up in the current session.",
ask:"Which mechanism fits best?",
a:["<code>fork_session</code>, to branch two independent lines of exploration from the shared accumulated context",
"Two fresh sessions, each rebuilding the codebase context from scratch",
"One session that explores approach A, then is told to forget it and explore approach B",
"A single subagent asked to evaluate both approaches in one response"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Forking is designed exactly for this: shared prefix context, independent divergent branches, cleanly comparable outcomes.",
"Correct outcome but wasteful &mdash; it discards 20 minutes of context twice and risks the two branches starting from subtly different understandings.",
"Models do not reliably forget on request. Approach A's reasoning contaminates the evaluation of B, which is the anchoring problem forking avoids.",
"Reasonable for a quick take, but the two analyses share a context and influence each other, and neither gets the depth of a dedicated branch."],
e:"Know the three session mechanisms and when each applies. <code>--resume &lt;session&gt;</code> continues a named session where you left off &mdash; correct when the accumulated context is still accurate. <code>fork_session</code> branches from shared context into independent lines &mdash; correct for comparing alternatives without cross-contamination. A <strong>fresh session seeded with a structured summary</strong> is correct when the old context has gone stale, for example after files changed on disk, because resuming with stale tool results is often <em>less</em> reliable than starting clean with an accurate summary.",
k:"<code>--resume</code> = continue (context still valid) &middot; <code>fork_session</code> = branch to compare alternatives &middot; fresh session + summary = context has gone stale."},

{i:35,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"Your billing subagent handles a dispute, then needs the customer's technical issue resolved as well. It writes a note in its output saying &ldquo;also please have technical support look at their router&rdquo; and returns.",
ask:"What is wrong with this handoff?",
a:["The handoff is expressed as free-text prose rather than a structured request the coordinator can reliably act on",
"Billing agents are not permitted to identify technical issues",
"The subagent should have called the technical agent directly to save a round trip",
"The customer should have been asked to open a second ticket"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Handoffs need machine-readable structure &mdash; target role, customer id, issue summary, what was already tried, recommended action &mdash; not a sentence the coordinator has to parse and may miss.",
"Noticing an out-of-scope issue is good behaviour; the problem is purely how it was communicated.",
"That would bypass the hub, creating spoke-to-spoke coupling and removing the coordinator's routing control.",
"Pushes work onto the customer and reduces first-contact resolution, which is the metric the system is judged on."],
e:"Structured handoff protocols are called out explicitly in the blueprint, for both agent-to-agent transfer and human escalation. A good handoff carries: the identifier (customer or case id), the reason for transfer, what has already been established or attempted, the recommended next action, and any relevant constraints. Free-text asides get missed, and when they are noticed they force the receiving party to re-derive context the sender already had &mdash; which is exactly the cost a handoff protocol exists to eliminate.",
k:"Handoffs and escalations are structured payloads: id, reason, what was tried, recommended action. Prose asides get dropped."},

{i:36,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"An architect proposes that the coordinator retry any failed subagent up to three times before considering alternatives, uniformly across all failure types.",
ask:"What is the main flaw in this policy?",
a:["It applies a transient-failure remedy to failures that are deterministic, so validation, permission and business-rule errors burn three attempts and still fail",
"Three retries is too few for a distributed system",
"Retries should always be handled inside the subagent, never by the coordinator",
"Retrying at the coordinator level breaks hub-and-spoke topology"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Retry policy must be driven by the error <em>category</em>; retrying a non-retryable error is guaranteed waste.",
"The count is not the issue. Retrying the wrong class of error zero times would be better than three.",
"Too absolute. Transient failures are best retried locally in the subagent, but the coordinator legitimately retries with a <em>changed</em> approach after a subagent gives up.",
"Coordinator-level retry is entirely consistent with hub-and-spoke &mdash; the hub owns error handling."],
e:"Error taxonomy drives recovery. <strong>Transient</strong> (timeout, rate limit, 503): retry, ideally with backoff, and ideally inside the subagent. <strong>Validation</strong> (malformed input): do not retry unchanged &mdash; fix the input. <strong>Permission</strong>: do not retry &mdash; escalate or take a different route. <strong>Business rule</strong> (refund exceeds policy limit): never retryable &mdash; explain and escalate. This is why MCP tool errors should carry an explicit category and an <code>isRetryable</code> flag: they let the caller pick the right recovery instead of guessing.",
k:"Retry policy follows error category. Transient &rarr; retry locally. Validation &rarr; fix input. Permission / business rule &rarr; never retry, escalate."},

{i:37,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:3,
q:"Your coordinator spawns subagents one at a time, waiting for each to return before spawning the next, even though the four research subtasks are entirely independent.",
ask:"How do you make them run concurrently?",
a:["Have the coordinator emit multiple <code>Task</code> tool calls in a single turn, so the subagents are spawned and run in parallel",
"Set a <code>parallel: true</code> flag in the coordinator's agent definition",
"Spawn one subagent and instruct it to spawn the other three",
"Reduce each subagent's workload so the sequential total is acceptable"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Multiple <code>Task</code> invocations within one coordinator response launch concurrently.",
"No such flag. Parallelism comes from how many <code>Task</code> calls appear in a single turn, not from a configuration switch.",
"Creates a mesh, hides three subagents from the coordinator's trace, and the intermediary adds a hop for no benefit.",
"Accepts the latency problem instead of fixing it, and reducing scope degrades the research."],
e:"Parallel delegation follows the same mechanic as parallel tool use, because <code>Task</code> <em>is</em> a tool: several <code>Task</code> calls emitted in one assistant turn run concurrently. Two prerequisites are easy to miss &mdash; the coordinator's <code>allowedTools</code> must include <code>\"Task\"</code>, and each spawn needs a self-contained prompt, since the subagents inherit no context and cannot see each other. Reserve sequential spawning for genuine dependencies, where one subagent's output is an input to the next.",
k:"Multiple <code>Task</code> calls in one coordinator turn = parallel subagents. Coordinator needs <code>Task</code> in <code>allowedTools</code>; each prompt must be self-contained."},

{i:38,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"Your support system routes 100% of conversations through a coordinator that classifies intent and delegates to one of five specialists. Analysis shows 70% of conversations are single-intent and fully resolved by the first specialist, but every one of them pays the coordinator's classification round trip.",
ask:"Which optimisation is most defensible?",
a:["Keep the coordinator but let it delegate on its first turn without a separate classification step, so routing costs no extra round trip",
"Remove the coordinator and let the user pick a specialist from a menu",
"Give every specialist all tools so no routing is ever needed",
"Cache classifications by user so returning users skip triage"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The overhead is the extra round trip, not the coordinator itself. Fold the routing decision into the delegation turn.",
"Shifts routing onto users, who typically describe symptoms rather than categories, and breaks down entirely for multi-intent messages.",
"Reintroduces the large-toolset problem that specialisation exists to avoid, and degrades selection reliability across the board.",
"Users' issues are not stable across contacts. A billing question today does not predict a technical question tomorrow, and stale routing is worse than none."],
e:"When you see a &ldquo;coordination overhead&rdquo; question, separate the <em>necessary</em> coordination from the <em>accidental</em> extra hop. Classification and delegation can happen in one model turn: the coordinator reads the message and emits a <code>Task</code> call for the right specialist in the same response. That preserves observability, multi-intent handling and the hub topology while removing the redundant round trip. The distractors each solve the cost problem by discarding a capability the architecture was built to provide.",
k:"Cut coordination cost by merging routing into the delegation turn, not by deleting the coordinator or broadening every toolset."},

{i:39,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"You are writing the <code>AgentDefinition</code> for a security-review subagent. Its <code>description</code> field currently reads: <em>Reviews things.</em> The coordinator rarely delegates to it, choosing the general code-review agent instead even for security-sensitive changes.",
ask:"What should you change first?",
a:["Rewrite the <code>description</code> to state precisely when this agent should be selected and how it differs from the general reviewer",
"Give the security agent more tools so it becomes more capable",
"Remove the general code-review agent so there is no competing choice",
"Add the word SECURITY in capitals to the coordinator's system prompt"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The description is the coordinator's primary basis for selection, and this one conveys nothing distinguishing.",
"Capability is not the problem &mdash; the agent is never being chosen in the first place.",
"Eliminates a genuinely useful agent to work around a metadata problem, and general reviews still need doing.",
"A blunt prompt hack that does not tell the coordinator <em>when</em> security review applies, and will over-trigger on everything."],
e:"An <code>AgentDefinition</code>'s <code>description</code> plays exactly the role a tool description plays: it is the selection signal. The same authoring rules apply &mdash; state the trigger conditions, the boundaries, and the contrast with similar alternatives. &ldquo;Use for changes touching authentication, authorisation, secrets handling, input validation or dependency updates; use the general code-review agent for style, structure and test coverage&rdquo; makes both agents selectable for the right reasons. Overlapping or vague descriptions cause misrouting between agents just as they do between tools.",
k:"Agent descriptions are selection signals, like tool descriptions. State triggers, boundaries, and the contrast with the nearest alternative."},

{i:40,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"Your synthesis agent produces a report that reads as fully authoritative, but one subagent had timed out and its subtopic was never researched. Nothing in the report indicates the gap.",
ask:"Which change most improves the trustworthiness of the output?",
a:["Require the synthesis step to annotate coverage explicitly, marking which areas are fully supported and which are partial or missing and why",
"Have the coordinator retry the failed subagent indefinitely until it succeeds",
"Have the synthesis agent omit any subtopic that lacks complete evidence",
"Add a disclaimer at the top of every report stating that content may be incomplete"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Explicit coverage annotation turns an invisible gap into a visible, actionable one.",
"Unbounded retries make failure a hang instead of a gap, and still produce nothing when the source is genuinely unavailable.",
"Silently dropping a subtopic is the same failure mode &mdash; the reader still cannot tell that something is missing.",
"A blanket disclaimer applies equally to perfect and broken reports, so it carries no information and readers learn to ignore it."],
e:"Reports that mix complete and incomplete coverage without saying so are worse than reports that admit the gap, because readers calibrate their trust on the confident-sounding prose. The expected pattern is per-section coverage annotation &mdash; <em>Visual art: full coverage. Music: partial, search agent timed out after two attempts. Literature: full coverage</em> &mdash; which travels with the content and tells a reader precisely where to be sceptical. This pairs directly with structured error propagation: the coordinator can only annotate what the subagents told it.",
k:"Annotate coverage per section: what is well supported, what is partial, and why. A gap you can see is worth far more than confident prose hiding one."},

{i:41,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"A coordinator has three specialists and receives: &ldquo;My order never arrived and I was charged twice for it.&rdquo;",
ask:"What is the correct orchestration behaviour?",
a:["Decompose into two tracked items &mdash; a shipping investigation and a billing dispute &mdash; delegate both, and confirm both are resolved before closing",
"Delegate to the shipping specialist, since the missing order is the primary complaint",
"Ask the customer which of the two issues they want handled in this conversation",
"Delegate to whichever specialist the intent classifier scores highest and close after it responds"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Multi-aspect requests are decomposed into separately tracked items, both delegated, both verified before the conversation closes.",
"Silently drops the duplicate charge &mdash; the more financially consequential of the two issues.",
"Pushes triage onto the customer and guarantees a second contact, directly harming first-contact resolution.",
"Highest-confidence single-label classification is precisely the wrong model for a message that genuinely contains two intents."],
e:"Two ideas from Domain 1 combine here. First, decompose multi-aspect requests into separate tracked items rather than collapsing them to a single intent &mdash; single-label classification is structurally unable to represent &ldquo;this message contains two problems&rdquo;. Second, verify coverage before ending the turn: the coordinator's closing check is that every enumerated item reached a resolution or a documented escalation. Note also that the two issues may be causally linked (a duplicate charge on an undelivered order), which is another argument for handling them in one conversation rather than splitting them.",
k:"Multi-intent messages: decompose into tracked items, delegate each, verify every item before closing. Single-label classifiers cannot represent multi-intent."},

{i:42,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"Your coordinator's context window is filling up because every subagent returns its complete working transcript &mdash; every search it ran, every page it read &mdash; alongside its conclusions.",
ask:"What is the appropriate fix?",
a:["Define an output contract requiring subagents to return only conclusions, evidence and source references, keeping working detail in their own isolated context",
"Increase the coordinator's context window and keep the full transcripts",
"Summarise each subagent transcript with a second model call before it reaches the coordinator",
"Have subagents write full transcripts to disk and give the coordinator a tool to read them"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Context isolation only pays off if the return payload is deliberately narrow. Define what comes back.",
"Buys time and forfeits the main benefit of the subagent pattern, which is that verbose work never enters the parent context.",
"Adds a model call and a lossy compression step to fix something better fixed by not producing the bulk in the first place. Summarisation also drops exactly the numbers and dates you care about.",
"Reasonable as a complement for auditability, but on its own it just moves the problem &mdash; the coordinator now needs to decide what to read, and reading it costs the same context."],
e:"Delegating to subagents protects the parent context only when the return value is scoped. Specify the output contract in the delegation prompt: the conclusion, the supporting evidence with claim-to-source mappings and dates, an explicit confidence or coverage note, and nothing else. The verbose exploration stays in the subagent's own window and dies with it. This is the same principle as trimming verbose tool results down to the handful of fields that matter &mdash; applied at the agent boundary instead of the tool boundary.",
k:"Subagents protect context only if their <em>return payload</em> is scoped. Specify the output contract; leave working detail in the isolated context."},

{i:43,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:3,
q:"You want the coordinator to evaluate the synthesis agent's draft and send it back for another pass when it falls short, rather than shipping whatever comes out first.",
ask:"What does the coordinator need for this loop to work?",
a:["Explicit quality criteria it can evaluate the draft against, plus the authority and mechanism to re-dispatch work",
"A higher temperature so its evaluations vary between passes",
"A fixed rule that synthesis always runs exactly twice",
"A separate evaluator agent that scores drafts from 1 to 10"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Iterative refinement needs a testable standard and a path back into the work; without criteria the loop has no stopping rule.",
"Variance is not judgement. Randomising evaluations makes the loop less predictable, not more discerning.",
"A fixed second pass wastes a pass on good drafts and stops one short on bad ones &mdash; the criteria are what should decide.",
"Numeric self-scoring is explicitly called out as an unreliable proxy; models are readily confidently wrong. It also adds a component without adding a standard."],
e:"Iterative refinement loops need three things: a concrete definition of done (every major sub-domain covered, every claim attributed, conflicts flagged, gaps named), the coordinator's ability to detect a shortfall against it, and a mechanism to re-route &mdash; back to synthesis with specific feedback, or out to a new subagent to fill a gap. The critical detail is that the feedback must be <em>specific</em>: &ldquo;the music section has no sources after 2023 and no coverage of streaming economics&rdquo; produces a better second pass than &ldquo;improve this&rdquo;. Self-rated 1&ndash;10 confidence is a recurring wrong answer across this exam.",
k:"Refinement loops need explicit quality criteria + specific feedback + re-dispatch authority. Model self-rated confidence scores are not a reliable gate."},

{i:44,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"A team wants to add a fifth specialist agent whose responsibilities overlap roughly 60% with an existing agent.",
ask:"What is the primary risk?",
a:["Overlapping responsibilities cause the coordinator to misroute unpredictably, and the same request may be handled differently on different runs",
"Five agents exceed the supported maximum for a coordinator",
"The two agents will consume each other's context windows",
"Overlapping agents cannot both be listed in <code>allowedTools</code>"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Overlap in agent responsibilities produces exactly the same misrouting pathology as overlap in tool descriptions.",
"There is no such hard limit. Reliability degrades with ambiguity, not with count alone.",
"Agents have isolated contexts by design; they cannot consume each other's.",
"Fabricated constraint. <code>allowedTools</code> governs which tools an agent may call."],
e:"The recurring cure for overlap &mdash; whether between tools or between agents &mdash; is to make the boundary explicit and non-overlapping: rename to reflect the actual distinct job, sharpen each description to name its trigger conditions and its exclusions, or merge the two into one agent if the distinction does not survive scrutiny. Non-deterministic routing is especially corrosive because it makes behaviour irreproducible: the same ticket takes a different path on Tuesday than it did on Monday, and neither run is a bug you can pin down.",
k:"Overlapping agents misroute exactly like overlapping tools. Fix with sharp, mutually exclusive descriptions &mdash; or merge them."},

{i:45,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:3,
q:"Your coordinator delegates to a subagent and receives back a 4,000-word analysis with no structure. The coordinator's next delegation quotes a figure from it incorrectly, mixing up which of two studies it came from.",
ask:"Which change most directly prevents this class of error?",
a:["Require subagents to return structured output that separates data from metadata, so each figure travels with its own source and date",
"Instruct the coordinator to read subagent output more carefully",
"Have the coordinator ask the subagent to clarify whenever it quotes a figure",
"Reduce the subagent's output limit so its analysis is shorter"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Structure is what keeps a value bound to its provenance through every subsequent handoff.",
"A vague exhortation to a probabilistic system, defending a factual invariant. It will fail at exactly the rate it fails today.",
"Adds a round trip per figure and still relies on the same unstructured text being re-read correctly.",
"Shorter unstructured prose has the same defect in a smaller package, and truncation loses findings."],
e:"Free-flowing prose is a lossy carrier for structured facts. When two studies, two figures and two dates coexist in a paragraph, any downstream consumer &mdash; model or human &mdash; can cross-wire them. Requiring structured output at the agent boundary (a list of findings, each with value, unit, source, date and a supporting quote) keeps the association intact through aggregation, synthesis and rendering. This is the same principle as using <code>tool_use</code> with a JSON Schema to guarantee well-formed extraction, applied to inter-agent communication.",
k:"Use structured output at agent boundaries. Every value carries its own source, date and unit &mdash; prose lets them drift apart."}
);
